mod module_builtin_name_parse;

// A name the compiler binds before any source is read -- a built-in type, a
// keyword -- taken for a global name. The built-in has no
// place in any source, so the one diagnostic is the declaration's, and names
// what the name already is. It once came in two halves, the second pointing at
// an empty line in a file called "init", the compiler's own starting point; for
// a keyword, whose node has no position at all, the compile crashed.
//
// The modules named after a built-in are u32.cone, a one-file submodule, and
// while/, a submodule folder: a module's name is bound where its folder or its
// file names it, so no name token is ever read that a keyword would refuse.
// Each module name is refused twice, at its 'mod' line: once in the
// submodule's own namespace, which holds its own name, and once in this one's,
// which holds its submodule's.
//
// Parse stage: the duplicate is found as the name is bound.

struct i8 {                 //~ ErrorDupName:8 "i8 is already the name of a built-in type"
}
